1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,715
A see-saw stays level only
when both sides weigh the same.

2
00:00:03,715 --> 00:00:05,990
A shopkeeper's scale works the same way.

3
00:00:06,390 --> 00:00:08,006
Swap cards with a friend.

4
00:00:08,006 --> 00:00:10,547
Give three, and you must get three back.

5
00:00:10,997 --> 00:00:12,897
Diya is helping in the kitchen.

6
00:00:12,897 --> 00:00:17,419
She weighs rice on a pan scale, adding
grains till the pointer stands straight.

7
00:00:17,769 --> 00:00:21,137
One side up, one side
down, until they match.

8
00:00:21,637 --> 00:00:23,651
Chemistry has a scale like this.

9
00:00:23,651 --> 00:00:26,677
Inside every redox reaction,
electrons get weighed.

10
00:00:27,327 --> 00:00:28,601
Here is the puzzle.

11
00:00:28,601 --> 00:00:31,414
In a redox reaction, one
thing loses electrons.

12
00:00:31,414 --> 00:00:32,954
Another thing takes them.

13
00:00:33,303 --> 00:00:35,044
Electrons cannot just vanish.

14
00:00:35,044 --> 00:00:37,763
Every electron lost must
be gained by someone.

15
00:00:38,163 --> 00:00:40,134
So counting atoms is not enough.

16
00:00:40,134 --> 00:00:43,158
We must balance the
electrons, and the charge too.

17
00:00:43,758 --> 00:00:46,805
Let's balance one reaction
on our molecule bench.

18
00:00:46,805 --> 00:00:48,995
Dichromate meets sulphite in acid.

19
00:00:49,345 --> 00:00:53,027
In dichromate, chromium has
oxidation number plus six.

20
00:00:53,027 --> 00:00:55,280
It ends as chromium plus three.

21
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:57,768
Each chromium gains three electrons.

22
00:00:57,768 --> 00:01:00,504
Dichromate has two
chromium, so it gains six.

23
00:01:00,904 --> 00:01:03,672
In sulphite, sulphur goes
from plus four to plus six.

24
00:01:03,672 --> 00:01:05,401
Each sulphite loses two electrons.

25
00:01:05,801 --> 00:01:08,027
Six on one pan, two on the other.

26
00:01:08,027 --> 00:01:09,086
The scale tips.

27
00:01:09,086 --> 00:01:10,790
So we take three sulphites.

28
00:01:10,790 --> 00:01:12,316
Three times two is six.

29
00:01:12,316 --> 00:01:13,216
Balanced!

30
00:01:13,598 --> 00:01:16,156
This is the oxidation number method.

31
00:01:16,506 --> 00:01:20,153
The total rise in oxidation
number must equal the total fall.

32
00:01:20,153 --> 00:01:22,409
Electrons lost equal electrons gained.

33
00:01:22,809 --> 00:01:23,925
Think of money.

34
00:01:23,925 --> 00:01:27,416
Whatever one person pays, the
other person must receive.

35
00:01:28,066 --> 00:01:30,124
Now let's finish the equation.

36
00:01:30,124 --> 00:01:31,236
Step by step.

37
00:01:31,586 --> 00:01:32,486
Step one.

38
00:01:32,387 --> 00:01:36,391
Write the skeleton, and mark the
oxidation numbers that change.

39
00:01:37,141 --> 00:01:38,041
Step two.

40
00:01:37,857 --> 00:01:39,656
Make the rise equal the fall.

41
00:01:39,656 --> 00:01:41,524
One dichromate, three sulphite.

42
00:01:41,524 --> 00:01:43,305
Two chromium, three sulphate.

43
00:01:44,055 --> 00:01:44,955
Step three.

44
00:01:44,930 --> 00:01:46,243
Balance the charge.

45
00:01:46,243 --> 00:01:48,202
The left side is minus eight.

46
00:01:48,202 --> 00:01:49,819
The right side is zero.

47
00:01:49,819 --> 00:01:52,473
In acid, add eight H plus on the left.

48
00:01:53,223 --> 00:01:54,123
Step four.

49
00:01:54,070 --> 00:01:55,911
Balance hydrogen with water.

50
00:01:55,911 --> 00:01:59,572
Eight hydrogen on the left,
so four water on the right.

51
00:02:00,322 --> 00:02:01,242
Step five.

52
00:02:01,242 --> 00:02:02,321
Check oxygen.

53
00:02:02,321 --> 00:02:03,950
Sixteen on each side.

54
00:02:03,950 --> 00:02:04,850
Done.

55
00:02:05,193 --> 00:02:07,972
Now compare acid and base side by side.

56
00:02:07,972 --> 00:02:12,307
In acid, we fix the charge with
H plus, and then add water.

57
00:02:12,707 --> 00:02:16,114
In a basic solution,
there is almost no H plus.

58
00:02:16,114 --> 00:02:19,842
So we fix the charge with
hydroxide ions, OH minus.

59
00:02:20,342 --> 00:02:21,444
Pause and think.

60
00:02:21,444 --> 00:02:23,656
A reaction runs in a basic solution.

61
00:02:23,656 --> 00:02:26,289
Which ion do you add
to balance the charge?

62
00:02:29,489 --> 00:02:31,326
Hydroxide, OH minus.

63
00:02:31,326 --> 00:02:33,701
H plus cannot last in a base.

64
00:02:34,351 --> 00:02:35,251
Remember this.

65
00:02:35,195 --> 00:02:37,127
Electrons lost equal electrons gained.

66
00:02:37,127 --> 00:02:39,153
Then fix the charge, then add water.

67
00:02:39,853 --> 00:02:42,653
Now, one worked example,
in a basic solution.

68
00:02:42,953 --> 00:02:47,041
Permanganate and bromide give
manganese dioxide and bromate.

69
00:02:47,541 --> 00:02:50,641
Manganese falls from
plus seven to plus four.

70
00:02:50,641 --> 00:02:51,869
It gains three.

71
00:02:51,869 --> 00:02:54,812
Bromine rises from minus one to plus five.

72
00:02:54,812 --> 00:02:55,936
It loses six.

73
00:02:56,686 --> 00:02:57,586
Three and six.

74
00:02:57,524 --> 00:02:58,851
So take two permanganates.

75
00:02:58,851 --> 00:03:00,111
Two times three is six.

76
00:03:00,861 --> 00:03:01,962
Now the charge.

77
00:03:01,962 --> 00:03:03,884
The left side is minus three.

78
00:03:03,884 --> 00:03:05,759
The right side is minus one.

79
00:03:05,759 --> 00:03:07,774
Add two hydroxide on the right.

80
00:03:08,524 --> 00:03:09,424
Hydrogen.

81
00:03:09,255 --> 00:03:12,236
Two on the right, so
one water on the left.

82
00:03:12,236 --> 00:03:14,111
Oxygen, nine on each side.

83
00:03:14,861 --> 00:03:19,803
Two permanganate, one bromide and
one water give two manganese dioxide,

84
00:03:19,803 --> 00:03:22,074
one bromate and two hydroxide.

85
00:03:22,724 --> 00:03:24,185
Now, the exam trap.

86
00:03:24,535 --> 00:03:27,759
Students see chromium change
by three, and stop there.

87
00:03:27,759 --> 00:03:30,013
But dichromate has two chromium atoms.

88
00:03:30,413 --> 00:03:33,894
Multiply the change per
atom by the number of atoms.

89
00:03:33,894 --> 00:03:35,610
Two times three is six.

90
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:37,775
Where is this asked?

91
00:03:37,775 --> 00:03:40,654
Boards ask you to balance a full equation.

92
00:03:40,654 --> 00:03:44,783
Entrance tests ask for one
coefficient, like H plus or water.

93
00:03:45,383 --> 00:03:46,534
Exam level now.

94
00:03:46,534 --> 00:03:50,124
Dichromate turns hydrogen
sulphide into sulphur, in acid.

95
00:03:50,124 --> 00:03:52,026
Chromium ends as plus three.

96
00:03:52,026 --> 00:03:56,640
In the balanced equation, what are
the coefficients of H plus and water?

97
00:03:56,640 --> 00:03:57,840
Pause and solve.

98
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:04,577
The answer is A.

99
00:04:04,577 --> 00:04:05,852
Eight and seven.

100
00:04:06,352 --> 00:04:07,983
Two chromium gain six.

101
00:04:07,983 --> 00:04:11,358
Sulphur goes from minus
two to zero, losing two.

102
00:04:11,358 --> 00:04:13,563
So take three hydrogen sulphide.

103
00:04:14,063 --> 00:04:14,963
Charge.

104
00:04:14,708 --> 00:04:18,442
The left side is minus two,
the right side is plus six.

105
00:04:18,442 --> 00:04:19,752
Add eight H plus.

106
00:04:20,252 --> 00:04:21,152
Hydrogen.

107
00:04:21,044 --> 00:04:23,775
Fourteen on the left, so seven water.

108
00:04:23,775 --> 00:04:26,192
B counted all fourteen as H plus.

109
00:04:26,792 --> 00:04:28,333
Here is your recap.

110
00:04:28,333 --> 00:04:29,915
Screenshot this card.

111
00:04:33,115 --> 00:04:35,302
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

112
00:04:35,702 --> 00:04:36,602
One.

113
00:04:36,206 --> 00:04:38,697
In this method, what must be equal?

114
00:04:39,497 --> 00:04:40,397
Two.

115
00:04:39,957 --> 00:04:43,578
In a basic solution, what do
you add to balance the charge?

116
00:04:44,378 --> 00:04:45,278
Three.

117
00:04:44,917 --> 00:04:47,857
Why does dichromate gain
six electrons, not three?

118
00:04:48,757 --> 00:04:53,205
Next time: what if we split a reaction
into two halves, and balance each

119
00:04:53,205 --> 00:04:54,105
half alone?

120
00:04:54,077 --> 00:04:56,666
We find out in the half reaction method.
